                <pb facs="0187" n="79" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg79"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8329: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-02-28T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 28,
                                1927—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 1—9:55
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>188. Following from American consul general, Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 27, 1 p.m. One of the three Standard Oil Company vessels, the
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Meihung</hi>, recently commandeered at
                            Ichang over a week ago, arrived in the upper harbor of Hankow this
                            morning loaded with armed soldiers. United States Ship <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Pigeon</hi> went alongside, took the vessel in
                            tow and anchored near United States Ship <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Isabel</hi>, removed the soldiers numbering more than one hundred,
                            retained most of the rifles and ammunition and informed officer in
                            charge that rifles and ammunition would be turned over on request from
                            Minister of Foreign Affairs or commanding general of this area against a
                            receipt. I have so informed Chen.</p>
                        <p>Arms and ammunition consists of the general [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >sic</hi>] American merchant vessel: 579 rifles, 9 cases small arms
                            ammunition, 9 rounds 3-inch field piece ammunition, 2 baskets leather
                            belts and miscellaneous gear, 3 sabres, 1 lathe and miscellaneous gear.
                            Troops were from 30th company, Ten[th] Army. Other two vessels were
                            released several days ago. Protest against the seizure was lodged by me
                            with Nationalist authorities 4 or 5 days ago.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
